1
00:00:30,350 --> 00:00:35,350
This is a report on operations, first core removal and second core loading,

2
00:00:35,350 --> 00:00:40,350
of the Organic Moderated Reactor Experiment, called the OMRE.

3
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:45,350
This reactor experiment, located at the National Reactor Testing Station in Idaho,

4
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:50,350
is an Atomic Energy Commission project conducted by Atomics International.

5
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:56,350
The OMRE is designed for a maximum fuel element temperature of 800 degrees Fahrenheit

6
00:00:56,350 --> 00:01:01,350
and organic coolant temperatures of from 500 to 700 degrees Fahrenheit with a

7
00:01:01,350 --> 00:01:06,350
coolant velocity of 12 feet per second. The reactor produces 12,000 kilowatts of

8
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:14,350
heat. 31 fuel elements with a total uranium-235 content of 25.5 kilograms

9
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:23,350
are used to provide operating reactivity. An important function of the OMRE is to

10
00:01:23,350 --> 00:01:28,350
test organic coolant behavior under conditions similar to those of large central station

11
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:34,350
power plants.

12
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:38,350
Another major function of this reactor is the testing of prototype fuel elements for

13
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:45,350
full-scale organic moderated and cooled reactors.

14
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:49,350
The OMRE began operating during September 1957.

15
00:01:49,350 --> 00:01:55,350
By March 1958, thermal stability predictions on organic fluid were confirmed by a successful

16
00:01:55,350 --> 00:02:00,350
three-week test conducted at reduced power with the bulk coolant temperature at 700 degrees

17
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:06,350
Fahrenheit.

18
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:11,350
A subsequent series of tests demonstrated the degree of radiation stability of the organic

19
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:12,350
coolant.

20
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:22,350
No significant decrease occurred in the overall heat transfer properties of the coolant.

21
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:28,350
Operation of the OMRE during this period proved to be simple.

22
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:33,350
Low activation of the coolant allowed access to the primary coolant system.

23
00:02:33,350 --> 00:02:37,350
The use of standard components and piping eliminated long delays for parts in need of

24
00:02:37,350 --> 00:02:40,350
repair or replacement.

25
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:45,350
Most of the OMRE shutdown time was scheduled for test program modifications.

26
00:02:45,350 --> 00:02:50,350
For example, during one of the shutdowns, the OMRE fuel elements were relocated within

27
00:02:50,350 --> 00:02:55,350
the core to allow higher power operation of the reactor.

28
00:02:55,350 --> 00:03:00,350
Based on the records of the dimensions of each OMRE fuel element built, hot channel

29
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:05,350
factors have been determined for critical points in all fuel elements.

30
00:03:05,350 --> 00:03:12,350
When suitable radio flux plots are obtained, the fuel elements in the core are redistributed.

31
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:17,350
All work of this type is done over the open reactor with the coolant level approximately

32
00:03:17,350 --> 00:03:20,350
15 feet above the core.

33
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:25,350
There are no permanent fuel handling or reloading facilities for the OMRE.

34
00:03:25,350 --> 00:03:31,350
A tractor-mounted crane is used to handle reactor components.

35
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:37,350
To remove irradiated fuel elements from the reactor, three casks were constructed.

36
00:03:37,350 --> 00:03:43,350
Each cask will transport one OMRE fuel element or one OMR test element.

37
00:03:43,350 --> 00:03:50,350
The casks are also used to transport in-pile corrosion specimen holders.

38
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:54,350
All radiation shielding is designed in conformance with government regulations and the casks

39
00:03:54,350 --> 00:04:01,350
have been approved for interstate shipment of radioactive materials.

40
00:04:01,350 --> 00:04:04,350
The casks have supplemental shields.

41
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:10,350
A fuel element with 1,200,000 curies of activity can be transported in a cask if all three

42
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:16,350
supplemental shields are attached.

43
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:22,350
This shows the casks being tested at an Atomics International facility in California.

44
00:04:22,350 --> 00:04:26,350
Each cask is removed from its cradle trunnion and placed onto an auxiliary shield which

45
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:30,350
is mounted on a pipe stand.

46
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:34,350
When permissible fuel heat generation rates and heat transfer characteristics have been

47
00:04:34,350 --> 00:04:38,350
determined, fuel element removal operations are checked.

48
00:04:38,350 --> 00:04:43,350
An OMRE fuel element head casting is used in this test.

49
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:49,350
The tool assembly is lowered and contact made with the fuel element head.

50
00:04:49,350 --> 00:04:53,350
When the assembly latch is secured to the lifting plug of the fuel element, the tool

51
00:04:53,350 --> 00:04:56,350
assembly is withdrawn into the cask.

52
00:04:56,350 --> 00:05:02,350
The cask bottom closure plug is then rotated and raised.

53
00:05:02,350 --> 00:05:07,350
After satisfactory completion of tests, the fuel casks and auxiliary shielding are shipped

54
00:05:07,350 --> 00:05:13,350
to the OMRE facility to remove a fuel element and an in-pile corrosion specimen during a

55
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:19,350
scheduled reactor shutdown in July 1958.

56
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:23,350
A work platform is used on the open reactor to permit placement of the fuel element in

57
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:30,350
proper position for removal. After removal of the work platform, a tractor mounted crane

58
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:37,350
lowers the frame and auxiliary shield onto four pipe columns around the reactor. Shock

59
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:43,350
absorbing pieces are attached to the pipe columns. The auxiliary shield is centered

60
00:05:43,350 --> 00:05:50,350
by guide pins through holes in the frame and corresponding holes in the reactor flange.

61
00:05:50,350 --> 00:05:55,350
The cask is lifted from the cradle assembly and lowered into the auxiliary shielding.

62
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:03,350
The bottom closure plug of the cask is lowered and rotated to open the cask.

63
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:09,350
Using safety lock pins to prevent accidental drop, the tool assembly is lowered into the

64
00:06:09,350 --> 00:06:16,350
reactor vessel. When contact is made with the fuel element head, the tool assembly latch is

65
00:06:16,350 --> 00:06:23,350
is secured to the lifting plug of the fuel element and the element lifted into the cask.

66
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:28,350
After the bottom closure plug is rotated and closed, the cask is lifted from the auxiliary

67
00:06:28,350 --> 00:06:40,350
shielding and lowered into the cradle trunnion. Clamps secure the cask in the cradle.

68
00:06:40,350 --> 00:06:45,350
The afterglow heat output from the hottest OMRE fuel element is reduced to about 3.6

69
00:06:45,350 --> 00:06:51,350
kilowatts one day after reactor shutdown. If the heat generation rate of an element

70
00:06:51,350 --> 00:06:58,350
exceeds 0.8 kilowatts, the cask is filled with organic liquid. Thermometers are installed

71
00:06:58,350 --> 00:07:04,350
in the cooling water inlet and outlet to check proper cooling of the element. An expansion

72
00:07:04,350 --> 00:07:10,350
tank for the organic is connected to the cask which is in a horizontal position on the trailer.

73
00:07:10,350 --> 00:07:15,350
The two cooling water systems for the cask, one of them an auxiliary, maintain fuel element

74
00:07:15,350 --> 00:07:22,350
temperatures as low as possible for experimental reasons. The cooling systems need not be used

75
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:31,350
with the less active fuel elements. The two casks shown here, one containing an OMRE fuel

76
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:36,350
element, the other containing an in-pile corrosion specimen, are transported to a hot cell on

77
00:07:36,350 --> 00:07:44,350
a 25 ton low bed trailer. Hot cell examination of the OMRE fuel element confirmed the satisfactory

78
00:07:44,350 --> 00:07:56,350
performance of this element in the reactor. By January 1959, the OMRE had completed 23,000

79
00:07:56,350 --> 00:08:01,350
megawatt hours of successful operation, and the reactivity of the fuel elements in the original

80
00:08:01,350 --> 00:08:08,350
core had diminished. A second core loading was initiated to provide higher reactor operating

81
00:08:08,350 --> 00:08:13,350
reactivity for continuation of OMRE test programs.

82
00:08:13,350 --> 00:08:21,350
The reactor was shut down and removal of irradiated elements in the original core was started.

83
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:26,350
For this operation, all used fuel elements from the first core loading are transferred

84
00:08:26,350 --> 00:08:32,350
in casks to a fuel element washing facility adjacent to the reactor.

85
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:39,350
The washing facility consists of two independent wash cells embedded into the ground.

86
00:08:39,350 --> 00:08:44,350
Lead filled adapter rings center the fuel casks in a vertical position and allow the

87
00:08:44,350 --> 00:08:49,350
safe insertion and removal of the elements from the wash cells.

88
00:08:49,350 --> 00:08:55,350
The washing cycle for each fuel element is a solvent wash, a detergent wash, and a water

89
00:08:55,350 --> 00:08:56,350
rinse.

90
00:08:56,350 --> 00:09:04,350
This washing cycle removes undrained organic material from the used fuel elements.

91
00:09:04,350 --> 00:09:09,350
After the elements are washed, they are transported in casks to a water canal at the engineering

92
00:09:09,350 --> 00:09:13,350
test reactor for storage.

93
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:18,350
When a cask is partially submerged to a safe shielding depth, the cask bottom closure plug

94
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:24,350
is opened and rotated.

95
00:09:24,350 --> 00:09:37,350
The element is lowered by the tool assembly and nested in the canal.

96
00:09:37,350 --> 00:09:42,350
The fuel cask is raised and given a water rinse before being returned to the OMRE for

97
00:09:42,350 --> 00:09:48,350
continued fuel removal and washing operations.

98
00:09:48,350 --> 00:09:53,350
After all used fuel elements are removed, the criticality control rod support structure

99
00:09:53,350 --> 00:09:57,350
is placed over the core vessel and the control rods and other associated

100
00:09:57,350 --> 00:10:00,350
components are checked for proper operation prior to proceeding with the

101
00:10:00,350 --> 00:10:11,350
second core loading. In April 1959 new fuel elements of the same type used in

102
00:10:11,350 --> 00:10:19,350
the initial loading were inserted into the core. Nuclear control is furnished by

103
00:10:19,350 --> 00:10:27,350
modified reactor control rod system and neutron counters. The temperature of the

104
00:10:27,350 --> 00:10:31,350
organic moderator coolant is maintained at 250 degrees Fahrenheit during the

105
00:10:31,350 --> 00:10:39,350
core assembly. Nine fuel elements are inserted into the core for the first

106
00:10:39,350 --> 00:10:48,350
fuel addition. Fuel elements are carried into the reactor housing one at a time.

107
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:52,350
The core position of each element is decided in advance.

108
00:10:55,350 --> 00:10:59,350
No more fuel than one half the difference between the estimated critical mass

109
00:10:59,350 --> 00:11:04,350
and the fuel already in the core is inserted during any fuel addition.

110
00:11:07,350 --> 00:11:10,350
The second fuel addition consists of six elements.

111
00:11:13,350 --> 00:11:17,350
The third fuel addition is comprised of three elements.

112
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:24,350
Each remaining fuel addition consists of one element.

113
00:11:25,350 --> 00:11:28,350
Subcritical multiplication counts are obtained after each individual fuel

114
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:33,350
addition.

115
00:11:33,350 --> 00:11:41,350
As in the initial core loading, critical conditions are achieved with the insertion of the twentieth element.

116
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:45,350
Additional elements are inserted for the loading of excess reactivity

117
00:11:45,350 --> 00:11:52,350
to about eight percent Delta K over K.

118
00:11:52,350 --> 00:11:57,350
To furnish data for the continuing research and development of the organic reactor concept,

119
00:11:57,350 --> 00:12:02,350
thermocoupled fuel elements are installed in the OMRE.

120
00:12:02,350 --> 00:12:06,350
Six of these elements are located in one quadrant of the core.

121
00:12:06,350 --> 00:12:09,350
Assuming the core flux to be symmetrical,

122
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:14,350
the temperature profile of the core can thus be inferred.

123
00:12:14,350 --> 00:12:21,350
Another advantage of locating the thermocoupled elements in proximity is the clearance of thermocoupled conduits from a large area of the core.

124
00:12:22,350 --> 00:12:27,350
This arrangement lessens the possibility of conduits interfering with the handling of OMR test elements.

125
00:12:29,350 --> 00:12:36,350
Three test elements of the type to be used in organic cooled reactor power plants are installed in the OMRE.

126
00:12:38,350 --> 00:12:43,350
They are instrumented to measure temperatures and coolant flows during reactor operation.

127
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:51,350
and will provide results on the performance of fuel elements being considered for full-scale OMR power plants.

128
00:12:54,350 --> 00:13:00,350
After completion of control rod calibrations, low power operating tests are performed

129
00:13:00,350 --> 00:13:03,350
and the reactor is brought to power in June 1959

130
00:13:03,350 --> 00:13:07,350
with an inlet coolant temperature of 600 degrees Fahrenheit,

131
00:13:08,350 --> 00:13:12,350
a maximum fuel plate surface temperature of 800 degrees Fahrenheit,

132
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:15,350
at a nominal power level of 11 megawatts.

133
00:13:16,350 --> 00:13:21,350
This proven and reliable organic moderated reactor experiment

134
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:24,350
is the basis for a continuing extensive effort

135
00:13:24,350 --> 00:13:29,350
to develop the organic reactor concept for small and large power plants,

136
00:13:30,350 --> 00:13:33,350
nuclear-powered ships, and processed heat plants.
